@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The body the legacy Site/Exceptions/RestrictedTransactionMode.aspx rendered (h1 + divider +
   p + "ui pink button" back link), extracted into a component so it can be rendered IN PLACE by
   AuthorizationOutlet for the
   Server.Transfer("~/Site/Exceptions/RestrictedTransactionMode.aspx") call site (checklist task
   56) AND reused verbatim by the routable page (task 95).

   THE BACK LINK (updated by Specialist 8 for task 95). The original href was
   "javascript:history.go(-1);". A javascript: url is a CSP-hostile legacy form, so the navigation
   is now performed through IJSRuntime (history.back(), which is history.go(-1)); href="#" plus
   @onclick:preventDefault keeps the anchor looking and behaving exactly as before. The class
   ("ui pink button") and the text (Titles.BackToPreviousPage) are unchanged, and because this
   component is rendered inside the globally interactive <Routes>, the handler is live on both the
   in-place denial path and the routed page.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
@using MixERP.Net.i18n.Resources
@inject IJSRuntime JSRuntime

<h1>@Titles.RestrictedTransactionMode</h1>
<div class="ui divider"></div>
<p>@Warnings.RestrictedTransactionMode</p>
<a class="ui pink button" href="#" @onclick="this.GoBackAsync" @onclick:preventDefault>@Titles.BackToPreviousPage</a>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    ///     history.go(-1) of the original anchor.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task GoBackAsync()
    {
        await this.JSRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync("history.back").ConfigureAwait(true);
    }
}
